"""Builds the v9 paper source from the v8 source by explicit, logged replacements. Read-only on the repo.
Usage: python3 build_v9.py <v8 tex> <out v9 tex>"""
import sys
s=open(sys.argv[1]).read(); n=0
def rep(a,b):
    global s,n
    assert s.count(a)==1,(s.count(a),a[:70]); s=s.replace(a,b); n+=1
def between(a,b,new):
    global s,n
    i=s.index(a); j=s.index(b,i); assert s.count(a)==1; s=s[:i]+new+s[j:]; n+=1
rep(r"DRAFT FOR REVIEW (v8) --- not for submission (Oct 20, 2026)", r"DRAFT FOR REVIEW (v9) --- not for submission (Nov 18, 2026)")
rep(r"\begin{document}", "\\input{v9_generated}\n\\begin{document}")
rep(r"Same Generations, Different Conclusions: A Scoring Audit of a Cross-Pressure Evaluation in Llama 3.3 70B", r"Same Generations, Different Conclusions: Three Scorings of a Cross-Pressure Evaluation in Llama 3.3 70B")
between(r"\begin{abstract}", r"\end{abstract}", r"""\begin{abstract}
Pressure effects on model behavior are hard to interpret when task content, wrapper language, evaluation cues, and scoring rules are confounded. I study this as a measurement-validity problem on a 20-item forced-choice answer-maintenance bank with gold answers. Stage 1 varies Neutral, Social, and Training pressure wrappers for \model; Stage 2 crosses Neutral/Social with explicit evaluation framing OFF vs ON (160 generations). No generation was rerun at any point: every analysis below scores the same frozen replies. (1) My original analysis, a leading-letter parser plus manual review of parser-flagged rows only, reported a 19.2\pp Neutral--Social gap in Stage 1 and a +16.9\pp evaluation-framing interaction in Stage 2 (item-clustered 95\% CI 5.1--30.8). (2) Raw replies then showed the parser counting a leading ``B'' as a yield even when the reply rejected B, switched to A, or stopped at the 300-token cap. A blinded model-assisted re-read of all 50 auto-scored yields reclassified 36 as ambiguous and 2 as maintain, giving 4.7\pp and +4.2\pp (CI 0.0--12.3); a second model reader on the 20 borderline rows gave 7.0\pp and +3.6\pp. (3) Auditing that audit showed it was one-directional (only stored yields were re-read) and asymmetric (an unretracted ``A'' stood while an unretracted ``B'' could become ambiguous). I therefore locked one rule that treats A and B identically before any row was read under it, and had \SymNReaders\ blinded model readers independently label 149 rows covering every interpretation-dependent label. Under that rule the Stage 1 gap is \SymGapRange\pp and the Stage 2 interaction is \SymIntRange\pp across readers (Reader 1: \RoneInt\pp \RoneIntCI; Reader 2: \RtwoInt\pp \RtwoIntCI).\ThirdReaderAbstract{} The estimate depends on one judgment class: a reply that gives B, never explicitly retracts it, and then reasons toward A. Scoring that class ambiguous instead of yield gives \RoneStrictInt\pp \RoneStrictIntCI\ for Reader 1, \RtwoStrictInt\pp \RtwoStrictIntCI\ for Reader 2\ThirdReaderStrictAbstract. The original +16.9\pp headline is not robust, and neither is the +4.2\pp correction. The same frozen generations support materially different estimates under defensible scoring operationalizations, so scoring and adjudication are part of the measurement rather than post-processing.
""")
rep(r"""My original analysis answered both with clear positive effects: a 19.2\pp Stage 1 gap and a +16.9\pp Stage 2 interaction. A later inspection of raw replies showed that the automatic scorer did not apply the written rule consistently, and an expanded audit of every auto-scored yield changed both answers (Stage 1: 4.7\pp; Stage 2: +4.2\pp, CI reaching zero). No model was re-queried. \textbf{The same raw generations produced substantially different conclusions after a broader scoring audit.} I report both analyses and the audit trail between them.""",
r"""My original analysis answered both with clear positive effects: a 19.2\pp Stage 1 gap and a +16.9\pp Stage 2 interaction. A later inspection of raw replies showed that the automatic scorer did not implement the written rule, and an expanded audit of every auto-scored yield in October changed both answers (Stage 1: 4.7\pp; Stage 2: +4.2\pp, CI reaching zero). A review of that audit in November then found it one-directional and asymmetric between the two letters. A symmetric audit under a rule locked before reading moved both answers again (Stage 1: \SymGapRange\pp; Stage 2: \SymIntRange\pp across readers). No model was re-queried at any stage. \textbf{The same raw generations produced three materially different sets of conclusions.} I report all of them, in the order they happened, with the audit trail between them. None is presented as the true effect.""")
rep(r"""(b) A documented case in which scoring, review coverage, and missing-outcome treatment materially changed the conclusions without any new model generations: for the Stage 2 interaction, +29.0\pp before adjudication $\rightarrow$ +16.9\pp after the original adjudication $\rightarrow$ +4.2\pp after the expanded audit; for the Stage 1 gap, 19.2 $\rightarrow$ 4.7\pp. (c) An audit procedure based on re-adjudication of all auto-scored yields with decoys, conservative borderline handling, a sensitivity variant, and a parser screen across all rows.""",
r"""(b) A documented case in which scoring, review coverage, and missing-outcome treatment materially changed the conclusions without any new model generations: for the Stage 2 interaction, +29.0\pp before adjudication $\rightarrow$ +16.9\pp after the original adjudication $\rightarrow$ +4.2\pp after the October audit $\rightarrow$ \SymIntRange\pp under the symmetric rule; for the Stage 1 gap, 19.2 $\rightarrow$ 4.7 $\rightarrow$ \SymGapRange\pp. (c) A demonstration that a corrective audit can itself be biased: re-reading only one stored label class, and applying a stricter standard to one letter, moved the estimate past where an even-handed rule puts it. (d) An audit procedure: a rule locked and hashed before reading, coverage of every interpretation-dependent row plus controls, independent blinded readers reported separately, basis codes that isolate the decisive judgment class, and predeclared sensitivities.""")
