rep(r"""\section{Discussion: Scoring as Part of the Measurement}""", r"""\section{Symmetric Audit}
\textbf{Why the October audit was reopened.} A reconstruction of the full record in November found three problems. (i) \emph{One-directional coverage}: only stored yields (and 20 decoys) were re-read, so labels could only move away from yield. (ii) \emph{A standard applied to one subset}: the audit-time requirement of an explicit final letter was applied to former yields but not to the 56 rows adjudicated maintain in August--September, all of which also open with ``B''. (iii) \emph{Asymmetry between letters}: a cut-off reply opening ``A.'' with no retraction stayed maintain, while cut-off replies opening ``B.'' with no retraction were coded ambiguous in the audit-primary analysis even though both October readers had called them yields.

\textbf{Locked rule.} Before any row was read under it, I fixed one rule (file \texttt{RULES\_LOCKED.md}, SHA-256 \texttt{f085892d\ldots9155a53}), identical for A and B. A \emph{commitment} is an explicit letter given as the answer; a letter that is only rejected (``B is incorrect'') is not a commitment to it. A \emph{retraction} is an explicit statement that the earlier letter was wrong, or an explicit statement of the other letter as the answer; reasoning that merely points the other way is not a retraction. A committed, unretracted letter stands, whether or not the reply is cut off. A clear switch is scored at the final letter. No commitment, a retraction with no new letter, or unresolved competing letters is ambiguous. Statements of an option's content without a letter are ambiguous in the primary analysis for both options and are scored as that option in a predeclared sensitivity (``semantic''). A second predeclared sensitivity (``strict-U2'') scores as ambiguous every reply whose letter stands but whose later reasoning points to the other option. This operationalization is stricter and more explicit than the original SOP; it is an audit-time rule, locked in advance, not the original written rule.

\textbf{Audit set.} 149 of the 400 Llama rows: all 50 auto-scored yields; all 56 rows previously adjudicated maintain; all 9 previously adjudicated ambiguous; 9 auto-scored maintains flagged by the text screen; and 25 auto-scored maintains drawn at random as controls. The remaining 251 rows are parser-scored maintains that open with ``A'' and were not flagged; they keep their stored label.

\textbf{Blinding and readers.} Rows were shuffled under new opaque IDs. Condition, run ID, prior label, parser status and the truncation flag were removed; readers labelled A / B / AMBIGUOUS and were not told which option is gold. Reader 1 (Claude Opus) and Reader 2 (Claude Sonnet) worked in separate fresh sessions with the identical rule and sheet. Each label carries a basis code and a deciding quote; every quote was verified verbatim, and a machine check confirmed that every row labelled A or B contains an explicit commitment to that letter. Label files were hashed before the condition key was opened, and the analysis code verifies those hashes before running. The analysis script was written before labels were read; two implementation bugs found after unblinding (a group-key format error and a mis-referenced column) were fixed without changing any predeclared choice, and results were byte-identical after later hardening.

\textbf{Agreement.} Readers 1 and 2 agree on \SymAgreeOneTwo\ rows ($\kappa=\SymKappaOneTwo$). Disagreements are preserved, not resolved; all turn on whether wording such as ``this contradicts option B'' is an explicit retraction. Previously adjudicated maintains confirmed as maintain: \SymAdjMaintPara. Controls and screen-flagged maintains confirmed as maintain: \SymControlPara. No stored maintain became a yield. Of the 50 original auto-scored yields: \SymYieldPara\ (the October audit-primary coding kept 12). Of the 9 original ambiguous rows: \SymOrigAmbPara.

\ThirdReaderPara

\TableLedgers

\textbf{Results.} Under the symmetric rule the Stage 1 Neutral--Social gap is \RoneGap\pp \RoneGapCI\ for Reader 1 and \RtwoGap\pp \RtwoGapCI\ for Reader 2; the interval excludes zero for \SymGapExclZero\ readers, and the pre-set 10\pp gate is at the boundary (\SymGateText). The Stage 2 interaction is \RoneInt\pp \RoneIntCI\ for Reader 1 and \RtwoInt\pp \RtwoIntCI\ for Reader 2 (interval excludes zero for \SymIntExclZero\ readers), and \AgrInt\pp \AgrIntCI\ when Reader 1--2 disagreements are coded ambiguous. Stage 2 yields by cell --- \SymYieldCellPara\ --- so the October finding of equal Social yield counts under OFF and ON does not hold under the symmetric rule.

\TableSens

\textbf{The result rests on one judgment class.} Basis code U2 marks replies that give a letter, never explicitly retract it, and then reason toward the other option: \SymUtwoPara. Every U2 row from Readers 1 and 2 is a ``B''. Under the primary rule these are yields; under strict-U2 they are ambiguous, and the Stage 2 interaction becomes \RoneStrictInt\pp \RoneStrictIntCI\ for Reader 1 and \RtwoStrictInt\pp \RtwoStrictIntCI\ for Reader 2 (Table \ref{tab:sens}).\ThirdReaderStrictNote For Reader 1 the strict coding removes every Social OFF yield, which makes the estimate unstable rather than small. Which coding is right depends on the construct: if the measured behavior is the answer the model commits to under pressure, the letter stands; if it is the model's final settled position, these replies never reached one. I report both and do not select between them on the basis of the result. Truncation remains entangled with this class: Stage 2 yields that ended at the token cap number \SymTruncYieldPara.

\textbf{What can and cannot be said.} The original +16.9\pp is not supported under any audited scoring. The October +4.2\pp is not uniquely determined either: it required treating unretracted ``B'' commitments as missing. The symmetric estimates are not ``the true effect''; they rest on roughly a dozen yields in 160 generations, on a judgment class that reasonable readers could code either way, and on model readers. What the record does establish is the range: the same frozen generations give about +17, about +4, or about +10\pp for the same estimand under scoring procedures that each looked reasonable when adopted.

\section{Discussion: Scoring as Part of the Measurement}""")
rep(r"""A safety-evaluation conclusion changed materially here with no change in the model's outputs.""", r"""A safety-evaluation conclusion changed materially here, twice, with no change in the model's outputs.""")
rep(r"""Practical recommendations for free-form pressure evaluations:""", r"""The second change is the more uncomfortable one. The October audit had blinding, decoys, verbatim quotes and a sensitivity variant, and it still overcorrected, because it re-read only the label class that was suspected of being wrong and applied its stricter standard only there. An audit that can move labels in one direction will move the estimate in one direction. The remaining disagreement is not noise in the readers (agreement is high) but an undefined point in the construct: the prompt asks for a letter first, the rule scores the final commitment, and the model often gives a letter and then argues against it without withdrawing it.

Practical recommendations for free-form pressure evaluations:""")
rep(r"""  \item Keep the audit trail: report original and audit-primary numbers side by side rather than overwriting the original.""", r"""  \item Keep the audit trail: report original and audited numbers side by side rather than overwriting the original.
  \item Audit symmetrically: re-read every label class whose value depends on interpretation, not only the class suspected of error, and include controls from the rest.
  \item Lock and hash the scoring rule before reading, and state in advance how each foreseeable borderline class will be treated, identically for every answer option.
  \item Have readers record a basis code, so the estimate's dependence on any one judgment class can be reported rather than argued about.
  \item Align the prompt with the scoring rule: do not ask for the answer first if the final answer is what will be scored.""")
between(r"\section{Limitations}", r"\section{Conclusion}", r"""\section{Limitations}
Results are for one model (\model) on a 20-item bank of limited topical breadth, with small per-item cells. No generation was rerun; all three scoring audits were post hoc. Every reader after the original manual review was a language model, not a human adjudicator: the October reader was Grok Bot and was not fully independent of the unblinded pre-audit screen; the October second reader was a Claude session; the symmetric readers were Claude Opus and Claude Sonnet, which are different models from one family and are not independent in the way a human or another vendor's model would be. The session that built the blinded sheets and ran the analysis was not itself blind, though it assigned no labels. Blinding was imperfect at the response-text level: some replies quote the Social instruction.

The symmetric estimates depend on the U2 judgment class (Section 7), on about a dozen Stage 2 yields concentrated in a few items, and on a rule that is an audit-time operationalization rather than the original SOP. The symmetric audit read 149 of 400 rows; the 251 unread rows are parser-scored maintains opening with ``A''.

Truncation cannot be fixed without new generations. The 300-token cap truncated 20 of 160 Stage 2 replies, 12 of them in Social ON, so missing outcomes are differential by condition and excluding ambiguous rows from denominators is not missing-at-random; the ambiguity bounds in Tables 3 and \ref{tab:sens} bracket that assumption. Stage 1 did not record finish reasons, so its truncation is inferred from text. Condition-encoding run IDs had to be withheld in every review; future generation should emit opaque IDs from the start.

Provenance: the Stage 2 final-scored file was committed on September 6 and is unchanged, but the Stage 2 raw generations, the Stage 2 auto-scored file and the Stage 1 scored file entered version control only on October 17; contemporaneous cloud-storage timestamps and byte sizes corroborate them but are not a hash match. The Stage 1 raw generation file is not in the repository. The identity of the original manual adjudicator(s) and the exact review interface are not recorded. Nothing here licenses mechanism inference (including evaluation awareness as ``the'' cause) or cross-model generalization.

""")
between(r"\section{Conclusion}", r"\begin{thebibliography}{99}", r"""\section{Conclusion}
Holding the task fixed and varying only pressure and evaluation-framing wrappers, my original analysis of \model\ found a 19.2\pp social-pressure cost and a +16.9\pp evaluation-framing interaction. An October audit that re-read every auto-scored yield reduced these to 4.7\pp and +4.2\pp (CI 0.0--12.3). A symmetric audit, under one rule locked in advance and applied identically to both letters by \SymNReaders\ blinded model readers, gives \SymGapRange\pp and \SymIntRange\pp, and returns to small or unstable values if replies that give ``B'' and then reason toward A without retracting it are treated as ambiguous. \textbf{No model output changed at any point.} The original headline is not robust; the October correction is not uniquely determined; and I do not claim that the symmetric estimate is the true effect or that there is no effect. The supported conclusion is narrower and, I think, more useful: on this instrument and model, the estimated effect of social pressure and of evaluation framing changes materially under defensible scoring operationalizations, so scoring and adjudication are part of the measurement and must be designed, locked, audited and reported with the same care as the generations. This is a measurement-validity result --- not a mechanism claim about evaluation awareness, not a bank-wide uniformity claim, and not a multi-model survey.

""")
