rep(r"""This paper is a concrete case on the scoring side: the generations are held fixed, while extraction, review coverage, and handling of unresolved outputs change.""", r"""For multiple-choice formats specifically, an instruction-tuned model's first-token choice often differs from the answer in its generated text \citep{wang2024}, and extraction strategies, including LLM-based extractors, can produce misleading comparisons \citep{molfese2025}. On the judging side, holding responses fixed while varying the grader and rubric shifts jailbreak attack-success rates substantially, leading \citet{arellano2026} to treat the judge--rubric pair as part of the metric, and \citet{messing2026} argues that conventional intervals omit variance from judge, prompt and sampling choices that can reverse conclusions. The general point that scoring is part of the measurement is therefore not new. This paper adds a documented case on a pressure evaluation: the generations are held fixed through three scorings, a corrective audit is itself shown not to be even-handed, and one named response class is shown to decide the sign of the estimate.""")
